<!-- ─── §01 ─── -->
<section id="s-intro" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Taxonomy</div>
<h2>Five Levels of Self-Improvement</h2>
<p>
  "Self-improvement" is not a single capability — it is a spectrum of five qualitatively different feedback loops, each harder to evaluate than the last. <span class="inf">inferred: this taxonomy is our own framing, not taken from a single paper</span> A model that polishes its own chain-of-thought prompt is doing something fundamentally different from one that rewrites its own training loop. Conflating them leads to both inflated claims and missed research gaps. <span class="inf">inferred</span>
</p>
<p>
  The taxonomy below organises capabilities by <em>what the model modifies</em> and <em>how persistent that change is</em>. Level 0 changes are ephemeral; Level 4 changes outlast a single run and compound across generations. <span class="inf">inferred: persistence framing is ours</span>
</p>

<!-- Step navigator: 5 levels -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">self-improvement-levels.py</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Click Prev / Next to explore each level</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="level-steps">
      <div class="step-box active" data-level="0">
        <div class="step-num">0</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Level 0 — Prompt &amp; Scaffold</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Rewrites its own system prompts, tool descriptions, chain-of-thought instructions, or retry logic. Changes last one session at most. Well-studied by STOP (arXiv:2310.02304) and OPRO (ICLR 2024).</div>
          <div class="step-code">Example: "Rewrite this CoT prompt to score higher on GSM8K; test 20 variants."</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-level="1">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Level 1 — Training Data &amp; Reward</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Generates its own training data, reward signals, or preference pairs. Outputs feed into a subsequent fine-tuning step. Covers self-play, RLHF automation, synthetic data pipelines.</div>
          <div class="step-code">Example: "Generate 10k math alignment examples; evaluate quality with self-reward."</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-level="2">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Level 2 — Architecture &amp; Hyperparameters</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Proposes architecture changes (attention heads, layer depth, activation functions) or tunes hyperparameters, then runs ablations to validate. This is the MLAgentBench / MLE-Bench space. Best AI: 37.5% success rate (Claude 3 Opus).</div>
          <div class="step-code">Example: "Increase attention heads 32→48, add RoPE θ=500k; retrain 100B tokens."</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-level="3">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Level 3 — Training Infrastructure</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Rewrites the optimizer, distributed training code, memory management, or GPU kernels. RE-Bench documented an AI writing a custom Triton kernel that outperformed every one of the 61 human experts in the study — the clearest documented instance of Level 3 success.<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15114">RE-Bench↗</a> (Note: the 12.4% speedup figure cited elsewhere in this post belongs to a separate SIA experiment on GPU kernel optimization, not to RE-Bench.)</div>
          <div class="step-code">Example: "Write custom FlashAttention Triton kernel; benchmark against cuBLAS baseline."</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-level="4">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Level 4 — Recursive Weight Update</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Updates its own weights such that the next generation of the model can perform Levels 0–3 better — true recursive self-improvement. <span class="inf">inferred: "true recursive" framing is ours</span> SIA (arXiv:2605.27276) approaches this by combining harness updates and RL weight updates in a closed feedback loop (+25.1% on LawBench).<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27276">SIA↗</a></div>
          <div class="step-code">Example: "RL fine-tune agent on LawBench; use improved weights for next harness iteration."</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="level-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="level-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="level-prog">Level 1 of 5</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  A key observation: <strong>evaluation difficulty scales with level</strong>. <span class="inf">inferred: our analysis</span> Level 0 improvements are easy to measure — run the new prompt on a benchmark, compute delta. Level 4 requires tracking improvement across multiple training runs, controlling for data distribution shift, and distinguishing genuine capability gain from overfitting to the evaluation metric used during self-modification. <span class="inf">inferred</span>
</p>
<p>
  The frontier AI labs have credible Level 2 results (agents that can improve a Kaggle submission given a few hours). <span class="inf">inferred: assessment based on MLAgentBench and MLE-Bench results</span> Level 4 remains largely aspirational — though SIA shows the loop is closeable<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27276">SIA↗</a> — the compounding improvement signal is weak across generations. <span class="inf">inferred: SIA only reports single-iteration gains; multi-iteration compounding is not evaluated in the paper</span>
</p>
</section>

