<p>
  The headline numbers need careful interpretation. MLAgentBench's 37.5% covers 13 tasks ranging from "improve CIFAR-10 accuracy" (where AI succeeds reliably) to recent Kaggle competitions (where AI scores 0% — the tasks fall after the model's training cutoff).<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03302">MLAgentBench↗</a> MLE-Bench's 16.9% is a bronze-medal rate on 75 Kaggle competitions — meaningful because it's measured against the full public leaderboard, but it also means the model fails on 83% of competitions (100% − 16.9%, rounded).<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.07095">MLE-Bench↗</a>
</p>
<p>
  The SIA numbers (+25.1%, +20.4%) are more impressive in kind than in scale: they represent genuine closed-loop self-improvement where both scaffold and weights update together.<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27276">SIA↗</a> But all three SIA benchmarks are narrow, domain-specific tasks — legal classification, GPU kernel optimization, and RNA denoising.<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27276">SIA↗</a> The open question is whether the same loop generalises to "improve the general-purpose language model itself." <span class="inf">inferred</span>
</p>

<div class="callout amber">
  <strong>What benchmarks are missing:</strong> None of the six benchmarks surveyed here test <em>multi-iteration compounding</em> — whether running the loop twice produces more improvement than running it once. <span class="inf">inferred from reviewing all six; none explicitly test this</span> Without that, we cannot distinguish "genuine self-improvement" from "one-shot performance on a difficult task." <span class="inf">inferred</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─── §03 ─── -->
<section id="s-time" data-code="time">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Time Budget Paradox</div>
<h2>AI Wins at 2 Hours, Humans Win at 32</h2>
<p>
  RE-Bench (arXiv:2411.15114) revealed something counterintuitive: AI agents score <strong>4× higher than human experts</strong> given a 2-hour budget, but humans achieve <strong>2× the AI score</strong> given 32 hours.<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15114">RE-Bench↗</a> This inversion is not a fluke — it reflects a fundamental difference in how AI and humans scale effort. <span class="inf">inferred interpretation</span>
</p>
<p>
  AI agents win on speed: they can generate, execute, and evaluate candidate solutions over ten times faster than humans at much lower cost.<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15114">RE-Bench↗</a> A human expert spending 20 minutes reading documentation while the AI runs dozens of ablations is a rough illustration of this dynamic, not a specific paper measurement. <span class="inf">illustrative: "20 minutes" and "50 ablations" are examples, not RE-Bench data</span> But speed without strategic direction hits a ceiling fast. <span class="inf">inferred</span>
</p>

