<p>
  The divergence after 8 hours can be interpreted through two mechanisms. <span class="inf">inferred: our interpretation of RE-Bench data; the paper does not state these mechanisms explicitly</span> First, <strong>diminishing exploration returns</strong>: AI agents sample solutions broadly but with shallow depth — they try many variants of the same idea rather than questioning the idea itself. <span class="inf">inferred</span> After many iterations, the marginal gain from another ablation diminishes toward zero. <span class="inf">inferred: "~200 iterations" from the original draft was not from the paper and has been removed</span> Humans, by contrast, use those extra hours to step back, reconsider framing, consult prior literature, and attempt qualitatively different approaches. <span class="inf">inferred</span>
</p>
<p>
  Second, <strong>long-horizon planning</strong>: a human expert's 32-hour run is not 16 sequential 2-hour runs. <span class="inf">inferred</span> It includes distinct phases — initial orientation, broad experimentation, synthesis, and targeted validation — a structure implicit in why experts keep improving beyond the 8-hour crossover point.<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15114">RE-Bench↗</a> <span class="inf">inferred: the specific phase breakdown and hour ranges are our construction; RE-Bench does not publish phase-level timing</span> Current AI agents lack the metacognitive structure to allocate time across phases — they treat hour 30 the same as hour 2. <span class="inf">inferred</span>
</p>

<div class="callout cyan">
  <strong>The Triton kernel exception:</strong> RE-Bench reported one case where an AI agent wrote a custom Triton kernel that outperformed all 61 human experts.<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15114">RE-Bench↗</a> The interpretation — that success here came from tight feedback and a narrow, instantly-measurable objective rather than long-horizon planning — is our own. <span class="inf">inferred</span> If correct, this characterises the regime where AI currently wins at Level 3: <em>tight feedback, narrow scope, measurable objective</em>. <span class="inf">inferred</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─── §04 ─── -->
<section id="s-loop" data-code="loop">
<div class="section-no">04 — The Feedback Loop</div>
<h2>Harness Updates + Weight Updates: Closing the Loop</h2>
<p>
  Two research traditions have tackled AI self-improvement in isolation. <span class="inf">inferred: "two traditions" is our framing</span> The <em>harness-update school</em> (STOP,<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.02304">STOP↗</a> OPRO<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.03409">OPRO↗</a>) rewrites the agent's scaffold — its prompts, tools, and retry logic — while leaving model weights frozen. The <em>test-time training school</em> updates model weights via reinforcement learning, while keeping the scaffold fixed. Each approach has a ceiling: scaffold improvements hit the limit of what the frozen weights can accomplish; RL updates only transfer when the next task resembles the training distribution. <span class="inf">inferred: ceiling analysis; SIA motivates this framing but the ceiling characterisation is ours</span>
</p>
<p>
  SIA (arXiv:2605.27276) closes the loop by combining both in a single Feedback-Agent iteration.<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27276">SIA↗</a> The key insight, stated by the SIA paper: <em>harness updates make the model agentic — but weight updates build domain intuition that no prompt or scaffold can instil.</em><a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27276">SIA↗</a>
</p>

