<!-- ─── §05 ─── -->
<section id="s-gaps" data-code="gaps">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Blind Spots</div>
<h2>Five Things Current Benchmarks Don't Measure</h2>
<p>
  Even when we combine all six benchmarks surveyed above, a substantial fraction of what we care about — when asking "can a model meaningfully improve itself?" — remains unmeasured. <span class="inf">inferred: our analysis of the gap landscape</span> Here are the five most significant gaps. <span class="inf">inferred: "most significant" is our editorial judgment</span>
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">capability-radar.py</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Radar chart — AI vs human expert profile across six dimensions</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center">
    <canvas id="radar-canvas" style="width:100%;max-width:460px;height:320px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div class="radar-legend">
      <div class="radar-legend-item"><span class="radar-dot" style="background:rgba(139,92,246,.8)"></span>AI Agent</div>
      <div class="radar-legend-item"><span class="radar-dot" style="background:rgba(6,182,212,.8)"></span>Human Expert</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>1. Compounding Improvement Across Generations</h3>
<p>
  Every benchmark measures a single pass: run the agent once, compare to baseline. <span class="inf">inferred from reviewing all six benchmarks; none publish multi-iteration results</span> None ask whether running the loop a second time produces more improvement than the first — which is the defining property of true recursive self-improvement. <span class="inf">inferred: this definition of "true" recursive improvement is ours</span> FML-Bench introduced an "Exploration Diversity" metric and showed that agents with higher diversity correlate with better performance,<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.10472">FML-Bench↗</a> but even this only measures one iteration. <span class="inf">inferred: FML-Bench does not run multi-iteration compounding loops</span>
</p>

<h3>2. Metacognitive Accuracy</h3>
<p>
  Can the agent correctly predict which of its candidate improvements will work before running the experiment? <span class="inf">inferred: this gap is not explicitly identified in any of the six benchmarks</span> This is a potential key bottleneck in long-horizon planning. <span class="inf">inferred</span> Current agents appear to treat candidate modifications as roughly equally promising, based on their undifferentiated exploration behaviour in RE-Bench<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15114">RE-Bench↗</a> and MLAgentBench<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03302">MLAgentBench↗</a> qualitative descriptions. <span class="inf">inferred: neither paper measures prediction accuracy explicitly</span> A benchmark that scores <em>prediction accuracy before execution</em> alongside final task performance would expose this gap directly. <span class="inf">inferred: proposed benchmark design; does not yet exist</span>
</p>

<h3>3. Generalisation vs. Benchmark Overfitting</h3>
<p>
  When an agent improves a model's score on a specific benchmark, is it finding genuine capability improvements — or gaming the evaluation? <span class="inf">inferred: general ML evaluation concern applied to self-improvement context</span> The distinction matters enormously for self-improvement: a model that learns to exploit the benchmark's scoring function is not self-improving in any meaningful sense. <span class="inf">inferred</span> Current evaluations rarely hold out truly OOD test sets that the agent cannot optimise against during the improvement loop. <span class="inf">inferred from surveying all six benchmarks; none describe a living-holdout mechanism</span>
</p>

<h3>4. Safe Self-Modification</h3>
<p>
  An agent that modifies training code, optimizer settings, or distributed training infrastructure is operating in a high-consequence environment. <span class="inf">inferred</span> Current benchmarks measure success (does performance improve?) but not safety (did the agent introduce subtle regressions, memory leaks, or behavioural drift in other domains?). <span class="inf">inferred from surveying RE-Bench, MLAgentBench, MLE-Bench, and SIA — none report safety regression suites</span> A comprehensive evaluation needs both. <span class="inf">inferred: proposed requirement</span>
</p>

<h3>5. Diversity of Self-Discovered Solutions</h3>
<p>
  FML-Bench's Exploration Diversity metric found that agents with broader hypothesis diversity achieve higher final performance.<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.10472">FML-Bench↗</a> We interpret this as hinting at a deeper structural issue: current benchmarks reward final score, not diversity, which may create an evaluation bias toward narrow, high-confidence agents over creative, exploratory ones. <span class="inf">inferred: interpretation of FML-Bench's finding; the "evaluation bias" claim goes beyond what the paper states</span>
</p>

<div class="callout green">
  <strong>The AI Scientist exception:</strong> Sakana AI's AI Scientist runs an open-ended loop across diffusion modeling, transformer language modeling, and learning dynamics, generating full papers at under $15 each.<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.06292">AI Scientist↗</a> It is the only system in our survey that addresses gap #5 directly. <span class="inf">inferred: "only" reflects our survey scope; other systems may exist outside it</span> On gap #2: the paper reports its automated reviewer achieves near-human performance on conference-score prediction, while acknowledging calibration limitations.<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.06292">AI Scientist↗</a> On gap #3: the generated papers are evaluated by a reviewer trained on the same ML literature they are drawn from — a structural circularity, though not one the paper characterises as a flaw. <span class="inf">inferred: gap #3 critique is our structural analysis of the design, not a stated limitation in the paper</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─── §06 ─── -->
<section id="s-framework" data-code="framework">
<div class="section-no">06 — Toward a Framework</div>
<h2>What a Rigorous Self-Improvement Evaluation Actually Needs</h2>
<p>
  Given the taxonomy and the gaps above, what would a genuinely informative self-improvement evaluation look like? Here are four requirements — in order of increasing difficulty — and a practical protocol for each. <span class="inf">inferred: this section is entirely a proposed framework, not derived from any published evaluation standard</span>
</p>

<!-- Step navigator: 4 evaluation requirements -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">eval-protocol.py</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Four requirements for a valid self-improvement evaluation</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="eval-steps">
      <div class="step-box active" data-eval="0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Requirement 1 — Controlled Baseline</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The "before" model must be fixed and reproducible. All improvement must come from changes the agent makes, not from stochastic variation in training runs. Use seed-fixed training, and test on a held-out distribution that the agent cannot see during modification.</div>
          <div class="step-code">baseline_score = eval(frozen_model, holdout_set)  # seed=42</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-eval="1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Requirement 2 — Measurable Delta</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The improvement metric must be defined before the loop runs — not chosen post-hoc. Use a composite score combining capability gain, safety regression tests, and OOD generalisation. Report standard error across at least 5 independent runs.</div>
          <div class="step-code">delta = composite_score(improved) - composite_score(baseline)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-eval="2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Requirement 3 — Multi-Iteration Compounding</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Run the improvement loop N times. Plot cumulative delta vs. N. Genuine self-improvement shows superlinear or at minimum linear growth. A plateau or regression after N=2 indicates one-shot task completion, not recursive self-improvement.</div>
          <div class="step-code">deltas = [run_loop(model_i) for i in range(N)]  # N >= 5</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-eval="3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Requirement 4 — Safety &amp; Invariant Monitoring</div>
          <div class="step-desc">After each iteration, run a battery of invariant checks: alignment evals, refusal rate, reasoning faithfulness, performance on previously-mastered tasks (no catastrophic forgetting). A model that improves on the target metric but degrades on invariants fails the evaluation.</div>
          <div class="step-code">assert all(invariant_check(model_i) for check in SAFETY_SUITE)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
