<h3>The Hardest Requirement: Non-Gaming</h3>
<p>
  Requirement 3 (multi-iteration compounding) implicitly demands that the evaluation metric not be gameable by the agent. <span class="inf">inferred</span> This is notoriously difficult in practice. <span class="inf">inferred: general ML evaluation concern, no specific citation</span> If the agent can inspect the held-out test set during training — even indirectly, by observing the eval pipeline's behaviour — it will find shortcuts. <span class="inf">inferred</span> The only robust solution is a <em>living holdout</em>: a continuously-updated evaluation set that the agent has provably never seen, generated by human evaluators who are themselves improving it in response to the agent's prior strategies. <span class="inf">inferred: proposed; not yet implemented in any published benchmark</span>
</p>
<p>
  This is expensive, which is why no current benchmark does it. <span class="inf">inferred from survey</span> But it is the correct bar. <span class="inf">inferred</span> Without a living holdout, "self-improvement" benchmarks are measuring "self-improvement plus benchmark exploitation" — and we cannot distinguish the two. <span class="inf">inferred</span>
</p>

<h3>Constructive Outlook</h3>
<p>
  The published benchmarks establish that Levels 0–2 are now tractable: MLAgentBench,<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03302">MLAgentBench↗</a> MLE-Bench,<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.07095">MLE-Bench↗</a> and RE-Bench<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15114">RE-Bench↗</a> all provide quantitative measurements. SIA shows the Level 4 loop is closeable.<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27276">SIA↗</a> The near-term research priorities are (a) multi-iteration compounding evaluation, (b) safety regression test suites that run inside the self-improvement loop, and (c) living holdout mechanisms that make the evaluation honest. <span class="inf">inferred: proposed priorities; not stated in any of the surveyed papers</span>
</p>
<p>
  Level 3 (infrastructure rewriting) is where the most surprising near-term gains may come from — the Triton kernel result<a class="src" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15114">RE-Bench↗</a> suggests that AI can already exceed human experts on tightly-scoped, measurable infrastructure tasks. As the scope of "tightly scoped" expands, this is where we should be watching most carefully. <span class="inf">inferred: prediction; not stated in RE-Bench</span>
</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>The recursive barrier:</strong> <span class="inf">inferred: conceptual analysis throughout this callout</span> The deepest challenge is that every framework for evaluating self-improvement is itself created by humans. A model that can improve itself at Level 4 could, in principle, also improve the evaluation framework — potentially undermining it. <span class="inf">inferred</span> The first sign of a truly capable self-improving system may be that it finds a way to score well on our evaluation while not doing what we intended. <span class="inf">inferred: this claim is not empirically established in any paper we surveyed</span> Designing evaluations that are robust to this is an open research problem at the intersection of AI safety and capability measurement. <span class="inf">inferred</span>
</div>
</section>

